en:
Let $C$ be unit cost and $P$ be sale price: $\begin{aligned}
C &\in \{\$5,\ \$10,\ \$15\},\\
P &\in \{\$20,\ \$25,\ \$30\}.\end{aligned}$ The gross profit per unit is then $\pi = P-C.$

p.src:
Suppose a shirt costs $C=\$10$ and sells for $P=\$25.$ Then the gross profit per unit is $\begin{aligned}
\pi &= P-C\\
&= \$25-\$10\\
&= \$15.\end{aligned}$ The simple unit-level return is $\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{ROI}
&= \frac{P-C}{C}\\
&= \frac{15}{10}\\
&= 1.5.\end{aligned}$ So before omitted costs, the unit shows a $150\%$ return on the unit cost. The lecture’s implied discipline is that this arithmetic is not enough by itself. A product margin becomes a business only if supply, storefront, and marketing work together.

